[00:18.82] The legend lives on from the Chippewa on down
[00:22.88] Of the big lake they called Gitche Gumee
[00:26.63] The lake, it is said, never gives up her dead
[00:32.19] When the skies of November turn gloomy
[00:38.16] With a load of iron ore, 26 thousand tons more
[00:41.64] Than the Edmund Fitzgerald weighed empty
[00:47.68] That good ship and true was a bone to be chewed
[00:51.18] When the gales of November came early
[00:57.65] The ship was the pride of the American side
[01:01.16] Coming back from some mill in Wisconsin
[01:05.16] As the big freighters go, it was bigger than most
[01:08.45] With a crew and good captain well seasoned
[01:12.96] Concluding some terms with a couple of steel firms
[01:16.43] When they left fully loaded for Cleveland
[01:20.50] And later that night when the ship's bell rang
[01:24.43] Could it be the north wind they'd been feelin'?
[01:39.26] The wind in the wires made a tattle-tale sound
[01:42.04] And a wave broke over the railing
[01:48.29] And every man knew, as the captain did too
[01:51.54] T'was the witch of November come stealin'
[01:57.73] The dawn came late and the breakfast had to wait
[02:01.25] When the Gales of November came slashin'
[02:05.80] When afternoon came it was freezin' rain
[02:09.29] In the face of a hurricane west wind
[02:29.17] When suppertime came, the old cook came on deck sayin'
[02:32.53] "Fellas, it's too rough to feed ya"
[02:36.64] At 7 p.m. a main hatchway caved in, he said
